data.verdict:
{
  "verdict": "analysed",
  "causes": [
    {
      "cause": "The mechanical check anchors on the cited LINE number, so any unrelated edit above the citation shifts the lines below it, the snippet no longer matches at that line, and a live bug is verdicted `fixed` and autonomously closed.",
      "severity": "critical",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.3: the line is a hint, never an identity; `fixed` requires the snippet to be absent from the WHOLE file. AC13 pins it."
    },
    {
      "cause": "The one-shot review property is stated in SKILL.md prose, so a wrapper under pressure to finish re-runs the reviewer on a reworded artifact until it approves — the exact failure one-shot exists to prevent, and instructions do not stop it.",
      "severity": "critical",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.6: the core records the gate verdict against (issue, contentHash) and refuses a second attempt for the same revision. AC14."
    },
    {
      "cause": "The skill shells `gh issue close` directly, bypassing the floor, the gate and comment-first in one call — structurally the same gap as rails-guard's un-gated Bash surface.",
      "severity": "critical",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.6: every write flows through the core; a guard test asserts the shipped SKILL.md contains no mutating gh invocation. AC17."
    },
    {
      "cause": "An issue with no parseable reference has no content-hash cache component, so its key degrades to updatedAt alone; code changes then never invalidate it and a `valid` verdict persists after the bug is fixed — reintroducing the decay the tool exists to detect, inside the tool's own cache.",
      "severity": "high",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §4: such issues are re-verified every run, or cached only as `unverifiable` — never as `valid`. AC16."
    },
    {
      "cause": "Comment-then-act is not atomic. A comment succeeds and the action fails (rate limit, revoked token, a human closing it first), leaving a `closing because…` comment on an open issue; every retry adds another comment.",
      "severity": "high",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.8: durable marker per (issue, contentHash); a re-run detects its own marker and resumes at the action. AC15."
    },
    {
      "cause": "The autonomy floor lives in a repo file, so anyone who can land a PR can set it to [] and unlock autonomous closing — a one-line PR is the whole escalation path.",
      "severity": "high",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.7: the profile is a trust root with rails-guard's asymmetry — narrowing is ordinary, widening is privileged. AC18. Human decision at P1."
    },
    {
      "cause": "Relations are O(n^2) — 370 issues is ~68,000 pairs. Exhaustive judgment is impractical; a cheap prefilter is similarity scoring, which the spec forbids as evidence.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.4: similarity is a candidate FILTER, never evidence, and the filter's recall is the reported ceiling on findable relations. AC19. Human decision at P1."
    },
    {
      "cause": "On a repo where few issues carry parseable references, nearly everything routes to `model` (expensive) or `unverifiable` (no conclusion), and a sweep that examined almost nothing reads as thorough.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.9: every run leads with its route distribution, the same honesty rule as `truncated`. AC20. Human decision at P1."
    },
    {
      "cause": "AC12 claimed the emitted set is consumable by issue-lanes while the spec explicitly does not modify issue-lanes, so the criterion tested a shape nobody consumes and later schema drift would break the handoff silently.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.9: the set carries a schema version and AC12 pins it against a committed fixture; issue-lanes adoption is a separate ticket. Human decision at P1."
    },
    {
      "cause": "A mid-sweep gh rate limit or partial failure leaves some actions applied and some not, with the cache possibly written, and a re-run compounds rather than resumes.",
      "severity": "medium",
      "status": "folded",
      "resolution": "Spec §3.8: a mid-sweep failure must leave a resumable state, never a half-applied one; the marker makes resumption idempotent. AC15."
    }
  ],
  "unresolved": 0
}

